    "## 5. Level and silence analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "The first pass that actually decodes the waveforms, because these measurements need sample values,\n",
    "not headers.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Frame-based RMS.** `frame_rms` slices the signal into overlapping 1024-sample windows (64 ms at\n",
    "16 kHz) advancing 512 samples at a time, and computes RMS energy per window. Framing rather than\n",
    "measuring the whole clip is what lets us locate *where* the silence is instead of only how loud\n",
    "the clip is overall. The strided-index trick builds all windows as one vectorised operation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What each metric captures:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Meaning | Why it matters |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `peak_dbfs` | loudest single sample | near 0 dBFS suggests clipping |\n",
    "| `rms_dbfs` | average energy | overall loudness; big spreads mean inconsistent levels |\n",
    "| `clip_frac` | fraction of samples at full scale | actual distortion, unrecoverable |\n",
    "| `silence_frac` | fraction of frames below threshold | mostly-silent clips waste the encoder window |\n",
    "| `lead_sil_s` | silence before first voiced frame | segmentation started too early |\n",
    "| `trail_sil_s` | silence after last voiced frame | segmentation ended too late |\n",
    "\n",
    "**The threshold caveat.** `SIL_DBFS = -45` is an *absolute* level, so a quietly recorded clip can\n",
    "register as mostly silence even though someone is speaking throughout it. That is exactly why the\n",
    "VAD cross-check further down exists — it decides speech versus non-speech from spectral structure\n",
    "rather than raw loudness, so the two methods fail in different ways and disagreement is informative."
   ]
  },
