   "source": [
    "### 6.4 Disagreement flags and audition\n",
    "\n",
    "Here the comparison becomes actionable. Each flag isolates a specific, differently-caused problem:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Flag | Condition | Interpretation |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `energy_missed_noise` | VAD says silent, energy said voiced | Background noise above threshold — the energy lead/trail numbers understate the real silence |\n",
    "| `energy_over_trimmed` | VAD says voiced, energy said silent | Quiet speech below threshold — trimming on the energy numbers would clip real words |\n",
    "| `vad_no_speech` | VAD finds no speech at all | Strong candidate for a genuinely empty or noise-only clip |\n",
    "| `very_fragmented` | many separate speech segments | Long internal pauses, or two utterances merged into one clip |\n",
    "| `vad_speech_too_short` | under 1 s of speech total | Too little content to justify a 30 s encoder window |\n",
    "\n",
    "The final table sorts by total disagreement so the most contested clips come first. **Listen to\n",
    "these before changing any threshold** — the numbers tell you the two methods disagree, but only\n",
    "the audio tells you which one is right.\n",
    "\n",
    "Practical rule for what to do with the outcome: if `energy_over_trimmed` dominates, raise\n",
    "`SIL_DBFS` (say to `-50`) or switch to a per-clip relative threshold, and trust VAD for trimming.\n",
    "If `energy_missed_noise` dominates, the recordings have a noise floor and VAD should drive trimming\n",
    "regardless. If both are near zero, the section 5 numbers stand and the cheaper energy method is\n",
    "sufficient for the remaining datasets."
   ]
  },
